namespace DrillSergeant;

internal static class SargeHelp
{
	public const string Text =
		"WHAT IT DOES\n" +
		"Drill Sergeant (Sarge) runs rotor-and-piston mining rigs: a rotor turns an arm of horizontal pistons, vertical pistons lower the drills, and the rig mines out a ring-shaped pit layer by layer. Each rig can be driven by Sarge itself (Plugin mode, no script needed) or by the Automatic Mining Platform script in a programmable block (Script mode), which Sarge sets up and commands for you.\n\n" +
		"CREDIT\n" +
		"The mining pattern, its settings and the safety ideas come from the Automatic Mining Platform script by Kezeslabas (Steam Workshop 1695500366: " + ScriptLink.WorkshopLink + "). Sarge's plugin mode is its own code doing the same job; Script mode runs Kezeslabas' script itself, untouched. Thanks, Kezeslabas.\n\n" +
		"THE PATTERN\n" +
		"Step 0 lines up: the rotor turns to the nearer end of its angle range and the pistons go to the start. Then the rotor sweeps from one end of the range to the other, the horizontal pistons move the drills one step outwards, the rotor sweeps back, and so on until the arm is fully out. Then the vertical pistons go one step down and the arm works its way back in (or pulls in first, if you tick that). When everything in reach is mined, the arm pulls back to the start and the drills go off. The rotor turns slower when the drills are farther out, so the tips always cut at the same speed.\n\n" +
		"PLUGIN MODE\n" +
		"Sarge sends each step once, as the same settings you would type into the terminal: a sweep sets the rotor's velocity towards its other limit, a move sets each piston's limits and velocity. The blocks then stop by themselves at those limits. In between Sarge only reads angles, positions and cargo on your PC, which sends nothing.\n" +
		"It only acts while you're online and the server would accept your changes: within 15 m of the rig (or anything joined to it), or linked to it by antenna. Out of reach, the step in progress finishes by itself and the rig stands still; it carries on when you're back. Progress is saved for each world and rig, so it also carries on after a reconnect or a restart.\n" +
		"Warnings pause the rig: a block gone, damaged, unpowered or not yours to use, or a setting Sarge sent that something else changed (another player, a timer or a script). If the rig hasn't moved for 90 s, Sarge says so.\n\n" +
		"SCRIPT MODE\n" +
		"For worlds where grids stay loaded without you (single player, or a server that keeps them), the script keeps mining while you're away. Subscribe to the script on the Workshop, build a programmable block on the rig, load the script into it (Edit > Browse Scripts) and press OK. Then in Setup: Mode Script, pick the PB, Save, Apply to script. Sarge puts the script's tags (/Mine 01/, /Hor/, /Ver/, /Inv/, /Adv/) into the block names and writes your settings into the script's settings in the PB's Custom Data, the way you would by hand. Set (the third button on the main page) makes the script read them and line up, Start and Pause send its commands, each as one normal Run with an argument. The state, step, progress and time left come from what the script shows on the PB's screen; while a script rig is selected, the PB's detailed info shows here too.\n" +
		"Continue at N (in Setup) sends \"set;N\" and \"start\", so the script carries on from where Sarge got to. The script only takes even steps and the same step lengths, which Apply writes for it. The script needs an advanced rotor.\n\n" +
		"SWITCHING MODES\n" +
		"Never both at once: switching to Script mode pauses Sarge first. Switching to Plugin mode sends \"pause\" to the script, waits until it has stopped, switches its PB off, then hands the rig to Sarge at the step the script reached. Sarge also refuses to drive a rig while a PB running the script for it (its tag in the rotor's name) is still updating its screen: that shows as Conflict.\n\n" +
		"THE WINDOW\n" +
		"The table shows every rig in this world: mode, state (Ready, Lining up, Mining, Moving arm, Paused, Out of reach, Cargo full, Finished, Warning, Conflict), progress, how deep the drills are, cargo fill and roughly how long is left. Start carries on from where it stopped, Pause stops rotor, pistons and drills where they are, Stop ends the job (pistons back to the start, progress back to step 0). Remove forgets a rig without touching its blocks.\n" +
		"Setup: pick the rotor from the list (rotors within 150 m) and Sarge finds the pistons and drills on its head side. It guesses each piston's job from the way it points; check them and change any with Job. The cargo pause watches the drills, a container, or all containers on the rig. Optional: an LCD of yours for the status, and timer blocks started when the rig finishes or the cargo pause sets in. 0 as a step length means worked out from the drills. Share inertia tensor ticks that box on the rotor and pistons at start, only where the world allows it and the game has Experimental mode on; the rotor tensor refresh is the script's fix for stalling rotors (off by default). Alerts: a chat line in Sarge's colour, a sound, both or neither, on your screen only.\n\n" +
		"WHAT THE SERVER SEES\n" +
		"Only requests a player can make by hand, each sent once when it's needed and never every tick: a sweep is the rotor's velocity (and turning it on), a move is each piston's limits and velocity, pause and stop switch blocks off. The LCD is written at most every 10 s, only when the text changed. Timers get the terminal's Start. Script mode adds block renames and one Custom Data edit at setup, and one Run per button. Nothing is stored on the server: the rigs and their progress live in a file on your PC.\n\n" +
		"COMMANDS\n" +
		"/sarge opens this window. /sarge chat off, quiet, normal or chatty sets how much Sarge comments. /sarge name and a name changes what Sarge's chat lines show under; on its own it puts it back. /tim opens whichever of our plugin windows you used last, and the list at the top left switches to another of our plugins. Settings can add Start/Pause and Pause to rig rotors' toolbar actions.\n\n" +
		"FILES\n" +
		"Settings are in DrillSergeant_Settings.txt, this world's rigs in DrillSergeant_<world>_Rigs.xml, both in %AppData%\\SpaceEngineers\\Storage.";
}
